e to keep up  with the massive influx of patients just  three days ago this wing was under  construction and it was empty  now there's not an empty bed in the  entire ward they've seen over 100  patients in just the last 48 hours she  has a femur fracture here on the right  side and another femur fracture on the  left side  both of carlin bernard's femurs snapped  in half as she tried to escape a  crumbling market  hers is just one of thousands of  harrowing stories from haitians  suffering this tragedy  she could feel the earth shaking and she  started to run  and as she started to run she was hit by  falling debris and there was a man that  was next to her who died who's crushed  by that debris and she says that god  blessed her she's alive  caroline bernard will have two metal  rods placed into her legs and the good  news tony doctors tell us she will be  able to walk again after her surgery now  that's a much different outcome than  many of us saw back in 2010 when many  crush victims often immediately faced  amputation tony so vlad let's talk about  2010 because your reporting was powerful  then it's powerful now what are the  biggest changes that you've seen in the  response in the emergency operations  there in haiti  so sadly tony from 2010 one of the  things that i remarked when i started to  see the video coming in on saturday was  i had a hard time telling the difference  between 2010 and 2021 because nothing  has been done to ensure the safety of  haitians in the wake of that devastating  2010 earthquake the other thing tony is  the roads in the entire time that i've  been coming to haiti over the last dozen  years the roads are still insecure so we  have to helo in to the disaster zone  because the roads are either impassable  because of fallen debris or because  they're controlled by bandits all of  these issues that we're talking about  tony are man-made the earthquake is a  natural disaster that every country  around the planet faces but many of the 